  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given an array AAA of length NNN.\n\nLet F(L,R)F(L,R)F(L,R) be the array you get from AAA after removing all occurrences of AL,AL+1,…,ARA_L, A_{L+1}, \\dots , A_RAL​,AL+1​,…,AR​. In other words, for each value in AAA between the indices LLL and RRR, you delete every occurrence of that value, including occurrences outside the range [L,R][L, R][L,R].\n\nA pair (L,R)(L,R)(L,R) is called good if 1≤L≤R≤N1\\le L\\le R\\le N1≤L≤R≤N and F(L,R)F(L, R)F(L,R) is sorted in non-decreasing order. In the case that F(L,R)F(L, R)F(L,R) is the empty array, we say it is sorted.\n\nCount the number of good pairs.\n\n**Example:** Suppose A=[1,2,3,5,7,6,8,6,5,8]A=[1,2,3,5,7,6,8,6,5,8]A=[1,2,3,5,7,6,8,6,5,8]. Then F(4,6)F(4,6)F(4,6) is the array we get after deleting all occurrences of the numbers 5,7,65,7,65,7,6. Thus, F(4,6)=[1,2,3,8,8]F(4,6)=[1,2,3,8,8]F(4,6)=[1,2,3,8,8]. This array is sorted in non-decreasing order, so the pair (4,6)(4, 6)(4,6) is good.\n\n###Input:\n\n\nThe first line of the input contains an integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first line of each test case contains an integer NNN, denoting the size of the array.\nThe second line of each test case contains NNN space-separated integers, denoting the array AAA.\n###Output:\r\nFor each test case, output the number of good subarrays in AAA, in a separate line.\n\n###Constraints\n\n\n1≤T≤1041 \\le T \\le 10^41≤T≤104\n1≤N≤2×1051 \\le N \\le 2 \\times 10^51≤N≤2×105\n1≤Ai≤1091 \\le A_i \\le 10^91≤Ai​≤109\nSum of NNN over all test cases doesn't exceed 2×1052 \\times 10^52×105.",
    "constraints": [],
